🧠 Approach (Short & Clear – Interview Ready)

👉 Problem: Subset sum possible hai ya nahi

Idea:

Har element ke liye 2 choices:
Include (take)
Exclude (not take)
Hum check karte hain kya kisi path se sum ban सकता है
Repeated work avoid करने के लिए DP (memoization) use किया

👉 State:

(index, currentSum)
💻 Code with Child-Level Comments
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    static Boolean isSubsetSum(int arr[], int sum) {
        int n = arr.length;

        // dp[index][sum] = -1 (not visited), 0 (false), 1 (true)
        int dp[][] = new int[n+1][sum+1];

        // initially sab -1 (means not calculated)
        for(int i = 0; i <= n; i++){
            Arrays.fill(dp[i], -1);
        }

        // start from index 0 and sum = 0
        int ans = help(arr, sum, 0, dp, 0);

        return ans == 1;
    }

    public static int help(int arr[], int sum, int n, int dp[][], int count){

        // 🎯 Agar required sum mil gaya → success
        if(count == sum) return 1;

        // 🚫 Agar array khatam ho gaya → fail
        if(n == arr.length) return 0;

        // 🚫 Agar sum exceed ho gaya → useless path
        if(count > sum) return 0;

        // ⚡ Already calculated → return stored answer
        if(dp[n][count] != -1) return dp[n][count];

        // ✅ Choice 1: current element lo
        int take = help(arr, sum, n+1, dp, count + arr[n]);

        // ❌ Choice 2: current element skip karo
        int left = help(arr, sum, n+1, dp, count);

        // store result (agar koi bhi true hai → true)
        return dp[n][count] = (take == 1 || left == 1) ? 1 : 0;
    }
}
⚡ Revision Notes (Exam Quick View)

👉 State:
dp[index][sum]

👉 Base Case:

sum mil gaya → true
array end → false
sum exceed → false

👉 Choices:

take → count + arr[n]
not take

👉 DP values:

-1 → not visited
1 → true
0 → false

👉 Time Complexity:
O(n * sum)

🧩 Dry Run (Very Short)
arr = [2,3,7], sum = 5

Path:
2 → take → sum=2
3 → take → sum=5 ✅ (answer true)
🎤 Interview Answer (Best Short Version)

👉 “I solved this using recursion with memoization.
For each element, I either include it or exclude it.
I maintain a state (index, currentSum).
If currentSum becomes equal to target, I return true.
To avoid recomputation, I use a 2D DP array.
Time complexity is O(n * sum).”

🔁 Counter Questions (Very Important)
❓ Q1: Why 2D DP?

👉 Because state depends on:

index
current sum
❓ Q2: Can we optimize space?

👉 Yes → use 1D DP (tabulation)

❓ Q3: Why not greedy?

👉 Because subset combinations matter, not just local choice

❓ Q4: What if negative numbers allowed?

👉 This solution fails ❌
Need different approach (offset / hashmap)

❓ Q5: Can we solve without recursion?

👉 Yes → Bottom-up DP (tabulation)

🔥 Final Memory Trick

👉 “Subset sum = Take / Not Take + DP(index, sum)”
